  "text": "Neither solution gives the correct constant, so the answer is NONE.\n\nThe problem requires the new bound to refine $n \\le 2m-3$ for every $k \\ge 3$. That means $2m - \\lfloor (k+c)/2 \\rfloor \\le 2m-3$, i.e. $\\lfloor (k+c)/2 \\rfloor \\ge 3$. At $k=3$ this needs $\\lfloor (3+c)/2 \\rfloor \\ge 3$, so $c \\ge 3$.\n\n- **Solution 1 ($c=1$)** gives $n \\le 2m-2$ at $k=3$, which is weaker than $2m-3$, so it cannot be a refinement. Its choice rests on a heuristic about parity and \"typical\" constants, not on a derivation.\n- **Solution 2 ($c=2$)** also gives $n \\le 2m-2$ at $k=3$. The solution itself shows this fails the refinement condition, then reverts to the answer 2 by appeal to an unverified \"intended answer\" from a source it cannot identify.\n\nThe only value consistent with the stated refinement is $c \\ge 3$, and $c=3$ is the natural candidate (it reproduces $2m-3$ at $k=3$ and tightens for larger $k$). I have not checked the source paper, so I cannot confirm that $c=3$ is the exact published constant, but neither proposed answer is correct.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
